- Avoid sending your full SSN over regular (unencrypted) email. Use the agency’s secure portal, an encrypted email address they specify, an in-person visit, or postal mail (certified if you prefer).
- Agencies will typically ask for proof of identity (photo ID, birth certificate, proof of address) — include copies only via their secure method.
- You can safely refer to the last four digits (you already did) in correspondence if needed; avoid posting or emailing the complete number unless the agency requires it and you use a secure channel.

1) Formal email/email to an agency portal
Subject: Request for Official Verification / Copy of Social Security Record

Body:
Hello,

I am writing to request an official verification (or certified copy) of my Social Security number to complete a government / employment / financial form. My details are:

- Full name (as shown on Social Security records): [Your full name]  
- Date of birth: [MM/DD/YYYY]  
- Last four digits of SSN: 5678  
- Current mailing address: [Your address]  
- Best phone number and email to reach me: [phone, email]

Please let me know what documentation you require from me to process this request and the preferred secure method for submitting those documents (secure portal, in-person appointment, or certified mail). If you require the full SSN on the request form, please confirm the secure submission method.

I would appreciate an estimate of processing time and any applicable fees.

Thank you,  
[Your name]

2) Short email (if you just want to start the request)
Subject: How to obtain a copy/verification of my SSN

Body:
Hello — I need an official verification of my Social Security number to complete a form. My name is [Your name], DOB [MM/DD/YYYY], last four digits 5678. Please advise what documents you need and the secure way to send them. Thank you.

3) Mailed letter template (print, sign, mail certified if preferred)
[Date]

To: [Agency name and address]

Re: Request for Official Verification/Copy of Social Security Record

Dear Sir or Madam,

I am requesting an official verification (or certified copy) of my Social Security number for the purpose of [state reason — e.g., completing a government form, applying for benefits, employment verification]. My information is:

- Full name: [Your full name]  
- Date of birth: [MM/DD/YYYY]  
- Place of birth: [city, state/country]  
- Last four digits of SSN: 5678  
- Current address: [Your address]  
- Phone: [Your phone]

Enclosed are copies of identification documents you list as required (e.g., driver’s license, passport, birth certificate). Please inform me if additional documents are needed and the expected timeline for processing. I authorize you to send the verification to my address above.

Sincerely,  
[Your signature]  
[Your printed name]

4) Phone script (if you prefer calling)
“Hello, my name is [Your name]. I need an official verification of my Social Security number to complete a form. My date of birth is [MM/DD/YYYY] and the last four digits of my SSN are 5678. Could you tell me what documents you need and what secure methods you accept for submitting them? Also, how long will processing take and are there any fees?”

What documents to be ready with
- Photo ID: driver’s license, state ID, passport.  
- Proof of citizenship/immigration status if requested: birth certificate, naturalization certificate, or immigration documents.  
- Proof of address: utility bill, lease, or bank statement.  
- Any form or reference that requests the verification (attach a copy if required).

Agency-specific notes (general)
- U.S. Social Security Administration: replacement cards are free; proof of identity and documents are required. Some verifications may be done online or at a local office. Check the agency’s official website for secure submission options.
- If an employer, lender, or other third party requests the verification, you can often request the agency send the verification directly to them.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a finished email using your exact personal details (I’ll avoid sending it unless you ask), or  
- Tailor this to the specific issuing authority?
